{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>You shall not make a covenant, etc.</b> It is difficult: if He had already commanded that they demolish and smash them, what place is there to command about making a covenant with them? Further, how can it be justified to make a covenant with their elohim? And if it is speaking of the worship of idolatry, what relevance does making a covenant have?" "<b>However,</b> the intention of the verse is to say that there is no prohibition against making a covenant with the nations, so long as they and their elohim are with them — but if they have renounced their elohim there is no prohibition. Therefore it says <b>you shall not make a covenant with them and with their elohim</b> — meaning, when their elohim are still theirs in covenant." "It further intends to say that one who makes a covenant with an idolater is as if he made a covenant with the idolatry itself, for two reasons: the first, because he will ultimately stumble, as the adjacent verse states: ״lest they cause you to sin, etc., for you will serve״ (Exodus 23:33). And the second, because the dimension of the idolatry clothes itself in its worshippers, and in this it turns out that he is making a covenant also with their elohim, may the Merciful One save us. And one should not say that this alone is what it intends, for if so it should have said ״לֹא תִכְרוֹת לָהֶם בְּרִית וְלֵאלֹהֵיהֶן״ (you shall not make them a covenant and their elohim), and then we would say that the intention is that if he made them a covenant, Scripture accounts it upon him as if, etc. Rather, from His mentioning the covenant after the mention of their elohim, it indicates also what we explained — that He prohibited only at a time when he has not undertaken not to worship idolatry, but if he has no elohah it is permitted to make him a covenant, just as they made a covenant with the Gibeonites. And the reason that Israel were particular afterward is because they of blessed memory said (Jerusalemite Shemit 6:1) that initially they were among those who rebelled against Israel through the dispatch of Joshua's letter, and they were liable to destruction under the king's commandment ״you shall not let any soul live״ (Deuteronomy 20:16)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:09.494175963Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}